# Poker Risk Lab — the cron FALLBACK for hosts without systemd.
#
# The supported scheduler is systemd (prl-backup.timer / prl-backup-verify.timer). Use this
# only where systemd is genuinely not available, and read what you are giving up first.
#
# Install with:
#
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 0644 deploy/backup/crontab.example /etc/cron.d/prl-backup
#
# NOT `crontab -u prl-backup`. That is what this file used to say, and it did not work: the
# lines below source /etc/poker-risk-lab/backup.env, which every other file in this directory
# documents as 0600 root-owned inside a 0700 root-owned directory — backup.env.example says
# so, prl-backup.service's EnvironmentFile comment says so, and README.md's install block
# installs it that way. As prl-backup the `.` would fail with EACCES, the `&&` chain after it
# would never run, and the result is the worst failure mode a backup has: nothing happens,
# nothing is restored, and the only sign is a line in a log file nobody reads until the day
# they need an archive that was never written.
#
# ## Why this runs as root, and what that costs
#
# systemd solves this properly. It reads EnvironmentFile as root and THEN drops to
# `User=prl-backup`, so the secret never has to be readable by the account that does the work.
# Cron has no equivalent step, so there are exactly two honest options:
#
#   (a) run the cron job as root, read the env file as root, and drop privileges for the
#       backup process itself — what this file does; or
#   (b) loosen the env file to 0640 root:prl-backup so the backup user can read it, and run
#       cron as prl-backup — which puts PRL_BACKUP_KEY and the DATABASE_URL password inside
#       the blast radius of the unprivileged account, defeating the reason they are 0600.
#
# (a) is chosen because it keeps the secret exactly as restricted as the systemd path does,
# and because the alternative silently weakens a file three other files in this directory
# describe as 0600 for a stated reason. If you take (b) instead, change the permissions
# deliberately and write down that you did — do not discover it from a EACCES.
#
# The cost of (a), stated plainly: cron runs `flock`, `sh` and the `.` of the env file as
# root, so a compromise of any of those three, or of this file, or of /etc/cron.d, is root and
# not merely the backup user. `runuser` narrows the window to the shell wrapper — node,
# pg_dump and everything that touches the archive still run as prl-backup, and the file mode
# on /etc/cron.d/prl-backup (0644 root:root, enforced by cron itself, which refuses
# group- or world-writable files) is what keeps the wrapper from being editable by anyone
# else. It is a genuinely worse posture than the timer. That is the price of not having
# systemd, and it is the reason the timer is the supported path rather than a preference.
#
# ## Installed as /etc/cron.d/prl-backup, not into a user crontab
#
# Two reasons. It does not clobber whatever root's personal crontab already contains, which
# `crontab -u root` would. And /etc/cron.d entries carry an explicit user column, so the
# account each line runs as is visible in the file a reviewer is reading — with `crontab -u`
# the answer lives in the install command, which is exactly how the wrong user got baked in
# here in the first place. Note that cron ignores files in /etc/cron.d whose names contain a
# dot, so the installed name must stay `prl-backup`.
#
# ## What cron does not give you, concretely
#
#  * NO PRIVILEGE SEPARATION FOR THE SECRET. The paragraphs above. This is the item that is
#    specific to this deployment; the six below are true of cron generally.
#  * NO CATCH-UP FOR A MISSED RUN. If the host is off, suspended, or rebooting at 02:30, cron
#    skips the run entirely and says nothing. The day with no backup is invisible until
#    somebody needs it. `Persistent=true` on a systemd timer is precisely this, and it is the
#    single biggest reason the timer is the supported path.
#  * NO PER-RUN RECORD. `journalctl -u prl-backup` shows every run, its output, its exit code
#    and how long it took. Cron writes to a mail spool that on most modern hosts goes nowhere
#    at all, which is why the redirection below is not optional.
#  * NO FAILURE HOOK. There is no cron equivalent of `OnFailure=`. Whatever you use to notice
#    a failure has to be built into the command line, and the `|| ...` below is a placeholder
#    for it, not an implementation.
#  * NO SANDBOX. Every hardening directive in prl-backup.service — ProtectSystem, PrivateTmp,
#    NoNewPrivileges, the syscall filter — has no cron equivalent. Under (a) above that is
#    worse than it sounds, because the unsandboxed part is running as root.
#  * NO OVERLAP PROTECTION. systemd will not start a oneshot unit that is still running. Cron
#    will happily start a second backup on top of a first one that is stuck, hence flock.
#  * A NEARLY EMPTY ENVIRONMENT. Cron does not read the shell profile. PATH is minimal, and
#    nothing sets DATABASE_URL or PRL_BACKUP_KEY, hence the explicit `set -a; . file`.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# The seventh field is the user, which /etc/cron.d requires and which is `root` for the reason
# written out at length above: only root can read /etc/poker-risk-lab/backup.env.
#
# `runuser -u prl-backup --` is the cron stand-in for systemd's `User=`: it execs the command
# directly with the environment the shell has already loaded, so the secrets survive the
# transition and the account's `/usr/sbin/nologin` shell does not matter. `su -c` would matter
# — it runs the command through the login shell and nologin refuses. If runuser is missing
# (it is util-linux; present on Debian and RHEL), `setpriv --reuid=prl-backup --regid=prl-backup
# --init-groups --` is the equivalent. If node is suddenly not found after the drop, it is
# ALWAYS_SET_PATH in /etc/login.defs resetting PATH; use an absolute path to node.
#
# Nightly backup, 02:30. flock refuses to start a second run while one is in flight; without
# --nonblock it would queue instead, and tomorrow's backup would begin the moment today's
# stuck one was killed.
30 2 * * * root flock --nonblock /var/lock/prl-backup.lock sh -c 'set -a; . /etc/poker-risk-lab/backup.env; set +a; cd /opt/poker-risk-lab && runuser -u prl-backup -- node scripts/backup.mjs create' >> /var/log/prl-backup.log 2>&1 || logger -t prl-backup -p daemon.err 'nightly backup FAILED'

# Weekly verification and prune, Sunday 04:00. The three steps are chained with && so a
# failure stops the sequence: pruning after a failed verification can delete the last archive
# that would have restored.
0 4 * * 0 root flock --nonblock /var/lock/prl-backup.lock sh -c 'set -a; . /etc/poker-risk-lab/backup.env; set +a; cd /opt/poker-risk-lab && runuser -u prl-backup -- sh -c "node scripts/backup.mjs verify --all && node scripts/backup.mjs verify --deep && node scripts/backup.mjs prune --apply"' >> /var/log/prl-backup.log 2>&1 || logger -t prl-backup -p daemon.err 'weekly backup verification FAILED'

# The inner `sh -c` on the weekly line is not decoration: `runuser -u user -- a && b` would run
# only `a` as prl-backup and then `b` as root, so the prune that deletes archives would run
# with more privilege than the verification that decides whether it is safe to.

# /var/log/prl-backup.log needs its own logrotate entry, or the thing that was supposed to
# protect the disk eventually fills it. It is written by root's cron, so install it 0640
# root:adm rather than leaving it to be created world-readable — the backup script logs
# archive paths, not the key, but there is no reason for every account on the host to read
# the shape of the backup schedule.
